lessons.body:
SwiftData is Apple's Swift-native persistence framework — the modern front door onto the same storage engine Core Data has always used. It replaces `NSManagedObject` subclasses and `.xcdatamodeld` files with plain Swift types and macros.

## Declaring the model

The `@Model` macro turns an ordinary class into a persisted type — every stored property becomes tracked, storable state, with no `@Published` or manual change-notification wiring required.

```swift
@Model
final class Question {
    var prompt: String
    var correctIndex: Int
    var topic: Topic?

    init(prompt: String, correctIndex: Int) {
        self.prompt = prompt
        self.correctIndex = correctIndex
    }
}
```

## Container and context

- **`ModelContainer`** owns the schema and the actual storage, on disk or in memory. You typically create one at app launch and hand it to the view hierarchy with `.modelContainer(for:)`.
- **`ModelContext`** is the working area — a unit of work where you `insert`, `delete`, and mutate model instances, then `save()` to commit. This app itself keeps a `ModelContext` for tracking progress through its own questions.

## Reading with @Query

`@Query` is a SwiftUI property wrapper that fetches model instances and keeps a view's list live: insert or delete through any `ModelContext` backed by the same container, and every `@Query`-driven view refreshes without you writing an observer.

```swift
struct ProgressList: View {
    @Query(sort: \Attempt.date, order: .reverse) private var attempts: [Attempt]

    var body: some View {
        List(attempts) { Text($0.summary) }
    }
}
```

## Relationships and delete rules

A relationship between two `@Model` types can specify a delete rule through `@Relationship(deleteRule:)`: `.cascade` deletes the related objects too, `.nullify` clears the reference and leaves them in place, `.deny` refuses the delete while related objects still exist. Getting this wrong either orphans data or wipes out far more than intended.

> KEY: `@Model` gives you the schema, `ModelContext` gives you the transaction, and `@Query` gives SwiftUI its automatic refresh — the same three roles Core Data always had, with far less boilerplate to write.

codeSnippet.code:
func importNotes(from context: NSManagedObjectContext) {
    let notes = try! context.fetch(Note.fetchRequest()) as! [Note]
    DispatchQueue.global().async {
        for note in notes {
            print(note.title as Any) // touched off context's queue
        }
    }
}

struct ProgressList: View {
    let attempts: [Attempt]

    init(context: ModelContext) {
        attempts = (try? context.fetch(FetchDescriptor<Attempt>())) ?? []
    }

    var body: some View {
        List(attempts) { Text($0.summary) }
    }
}

@Model
final class Topic {
    var name: String
    var questions: [Question] = []
    init(name: String) { self.name = name }
}

@Model
final class Question {
    var prompt: String
    var topic: Topic?
    init(prompt: String) { self.prompt = prompt }
}

func record(_ attempt: Attempt, in context: ModelContext) {
    context.insert(attempt)
}

func summaries(for context: NSManagedObjectContext) throws -> [String] {
    let notes = try context.fetch(Note.fetchRequest()) as! [Note]
    return notes.map { note in
        // each access below can trigger its own round trip to the store
        "\(note.title ?? ""): \(note.author?.name ?? "unknown")"
    }
}

@Model
final class Topic {
    @Relationship(deleteRule: .cascade) var questions: [Question] = []
}

@Model
final class Question {
    @Relationship(deleteRule: .cascade) var attempts: [Attempt] = []
}

